<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Today Is Tuesday! – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All Week Long!</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Today Is Tuesday!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases. Interactive: Respond to simple conversations on familiar topics by using gestures or by pointing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize familiar words related to days of the week spoken slowly (e.g., "Monday." "Today is Monday." "It's Monday."). Can respond to the basic days of the week and weekend by pointing at the word or position on a basic calendar. Can recognize and respond to questions about the days of the week using basic phrases (e.g., "Is today Tuesday?" "No, it is Saturday." "It is the weekend.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, everyone!" and starts a simple "Days of the Week" song (e.g., to the tune of "Twinkle, Twinkle Little Star"). The teacher uses large flashcards with each day of the week, pointing to them as they are sung. Students listen and clap along. The teacher asks, "What day is today?" and points to the relevant flashcard.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher slowly and clearly pronounces each day of the week, displaying the corresponding flashcard. "Monday. Today is Monday." "Tuesday. Today is Tuesday." The teacher uses a clear, exaggerated pronunciation for /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day," pointing to their mouth. Students listen and repeat chorally. The teacher demonstrates simple actions for each day (e.g., pretending to read a book for Monday, run for Saturday).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard and asks, "Is this Monday?" (Students respond with gestures: thumbs up/down or "Yes!"/"No!"). The teacher then says a day, "Show me Tuesday," and students point to the correct flashcard on the board or held by the teacher. "Which one is 'weekend'?" (Students point).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful flashcards for each day of the week with simple activity images (e.g., Monday: school, Tuesday: park, Saturday: play). A short, teacher-narrated audio story or dialogue about a child's week, speaking slowly and clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Identifying words for days and weekends by using or pointing to them. Listening: Receptive: Recognize familiar words and basic phrases related to days and activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher says, "Listen to the story. What is the story about? A cat? Or a week?" Students point to a picture of a week or a cat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher plays or narrates a short sentence, e.g., "On Monday, Lily goes to school." Students listen and point to the "Monday" flashcard with the school image. The teacher repeats for other days and activities, using vocabulary like 'relaxing', 'exciting', 'busy', 'quiet'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Match:</b> The teacher holds up two flashcards (e.g., Tuesday and Saturday) and says, "On Saturday, I play." Students point to the correct flashcard. "Today is Tuesday." Students point to Tuesday.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Grammar: Noun + verb + noun ("Today is Monday."). Vocabulary: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday, weekend, day, week, today. Pronunciation: /w/ in "weekend" and /d/ in "day."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Listen and Repeat (Choral & Individual):</b> The teacher says, "Today is Monday." Students listen and repeat chorally. Then, the teacher points to individual students for them to repeat. The teacher focuses on clear pronunciation of "today" and the days of the week, reminding students of /d/ and /w/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Listening Bingo:</b> Each student has a bingo card with 6-8 days of the week (pictures or words) in different squares. The teacher calls out days slowly and clearly (e.g., "Listen! Tuesday!"). Students listen and put a counter on the correct day. The first student to get three in a row (horizontally, vertically, or diagonally) shouts "Bingo!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. "Teacher Says" (TPR with Days):</b> The teacher gives commands related to days and actions. "Teacher says, touch Monday!" (Students point to the Monday flashcard or the word on their desk). "Teacher says, jump for Saturday!" (Students listen and jump). "Teacher says, whisper 'Wednesday'." This encourages active listening and physical response.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All Week Long!".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Days of the Week Schedule" Listening Game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher displays a large, simple weekly calendar on the board with spaces for each day. Students have small individual calendars or worksheets with the days of the week. The teacher describes a simple activity for a specific day using "Today is..." or "On [day], I..." The students listen carefully and mark the correct day on their individual calendar or point to the correct day on the board. The activity encourages listening for the day and connecting it to a simple routine or feeling (e.g., "On Monday, it is school day. It is busy." "On Saturday, it is relaxing!").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says, "Today is Tuesday! Today we go to the park." (Students point to Tuesday on their calendar).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher continues with other days and simple activities/feelings: "On Friday, we draw pictures." "Sunday is quiet." "It is the weekend!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks simple WH-questions: "What day is today?" (pointing to Tuesday). Students listen and point to the "Tuesday" flashcard. "Is today Saturday?" (Students listen and respond with a head shake for "no" or point to the correct day).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic: Teacher models polite expressions: "Please listen carefully." "Thank you."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist for listening comprehension and participation. Visual "Listen and Circle" worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures representing each day of the week (or the words themselves). The teacher says a day (e.g., "Circle Monday."), and students listen and circle the correct day. The teacher repeats for 3-4 days and for "weekend."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly circling the day spoken by the teacher.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation in listening activities (pointing, responding with gestures).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Recognizing key vocabulary (days, weekend).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students to show a thumbs up if they can hear the days of the week, and thumbs down if it's still hard. Alternatively, they draw a happy face if they understood the days, or a confused face if they need more help.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What day was easy for you to hear today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you like to listen to today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this listening lesson? (e.g., TPR, flashcards, specific pronunciation focus).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for next time? (e.g., more repetition, slower pace, different types of visuals).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the students engaged, especially the 1st graders, throughout the listening tasks? How can I scaffold better for those who struggled?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 2: Reading Comprehension), students will see the written form of the days of the week that they learned to recognize by listening today. They will practice identifying these words in simple texts and on a calendar.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3 minutes</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>